Multi-field post-processing works: after successful character filtering, there are at most \(J_f\) obstruction bits.  
The row target grows to \(L_N[2/5,a+\sigma_F+o(1)]\); the single-field cost constant does not carry over.  
The extra height floor repairs rejection absorption, provided its bound is conditional on the fixed rational data.  
The prime-multiplier design still depends on Lemma 8.5′; the published alternatives checked below do not directly replace it.  
An integer-multiplier variant admits an elementary residue-averaging argument that removes that dependency.

## 1. Complete multi-field post-processing

Write
\[
D=\frac{\log N}{\log\log N},\qquad
\lambda=\log\log N,\qquad
\Lambda=D^{2/5}\lambda.
\]
All \(L\)-exponents below mean exponents relative to \(e^\Lambda\).

The post-processing result is independent of the relation-supply argument. Its hypotheses are explicit, verifiable row certificates and accepted odd-degree fields. I use the notation of [pp], but distinguish the shared rational form \(G\) from the monic defining polynomials.

### Lemma 1 — Multi-field row identity and matrix

Let \(N\) be odd, with no prime factor at most \(B\). Fix \(J\) accepted fields
\[
f_j\in\mathbb Z[x],\qquad \deg f_j=d\text{ odd},\qquad f_j(m)\equiv0\pmod N,
\]
where
\[
m=g_0g_1^{-1}\pmod N.
\]
Put
\[
t_j=\operatorname{lc}(f_j),\quad
K_j=\mathbb Q(\alpha_j),\quad
\beta_j=t_j\alpha_j,\quad
h_j(T)=t_j^{d-1}f_j(T/t_j).
\]
Assume that \(f_j\) is primitive and irreducible and that
\[
\gcd\!\left(g_1t_jf_j'(m),N\right)=1.
\]

There is a homomorphism
\[
\psi_j:\mathbb Z[\beta_j]\longrightarrow\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z,
\qquad \beta_j\longmapsto t_jm.
\]
These are separate homomorphisms with the same rational specialization \(\alpha_j\mapsto m\).

An atom assigned to field \(j\) consists of \(P=(a,b)\), a special prime \(\ell\), and verified factorizations satisfying
\[
G(P)=g_1a-g_0b=\sigma_P\ell n_P,\qquad \sigma_P\in\{\pm1\},
\]
where \(n_P>0\) and \(F_j(P)\ne0\) are \(B\)-smooth. Define
\[
z_{j,P}=t_ja-b\beta_j.
\]
Then
\[
\operatorname{Norm}(z_{j,P})=t_j^{d-1}F_j(P),
\qquad
\psi_j(z_{j,P})=\frac{t_jG(P)}{g_1}.
\]

For a Dixon row \(i\), let \(e_{i,j,P}\in\mathbb Z\) be its signed atom multiplicities. There are at most \(2k\) atoms, counted with multiplicity. Put
\[
E_{ij}=\sum_Pe_{i,j,P},\qquad E_i=\sum_jE_{ij},
\]
and
\[
q_i=\operatorname{sgn}(u_iv_i)
       \prod_{j,P}\left(\frac{\sigma_Pg_1}{n_P}\right)^{e_{i,j,P}}.
\]
Its verified row identity is
\[
R_i\equiv
q_i\prod_jt_j^{-E_{ij}}
       \prod_{j,P}\psi_j(z_{j,P})^{e_{i,j,P}}\pmod N. \tag{1}
\]

The binary relation matrix has these columns:

| Columns | Entry in row \(i\) |
|---|---|
| Shared rational sign | Sign bit of \(q_i\) |
| Shared rational prime \(p\le B\) | \(\sum_{j,P}e_{i,j,P}v_p(n_P)\pmod2\) |
| Field \(j\), parity | \(E_{ij}\pmod2\) |
| Field \(j\), good prime ideal \((r,s)\) | \(\sum_Pe_{i,j,P}[a_P\equiv b_Ps\pmod r]v_r(F_j(P))\pmod2\) |
| Field \(j\), quadratic character \(\chi\) | \(\sum_Pe_{i,j,P}(1-\chi(a_P-b_P\alpha_j))/2\pmod2\) |

Here the ideal columns range over
\[
r\le B,\quad r\nmid \mathcal D_j:=t_j\operatorname{disc}(f_j),
\quad f_j(s)\equiv0\pmod r.
\]

**Proof.** Homogeneity gives \(f_j(m)\equiv0\), hence \(h_j(t_jm)\equiv0\), proving that \(\psi_j\) is well-defined. The norm and specialization formulas follow immediately. Substitution of
\[
\ell\equiv \sigma_Pg_1t_j^{-1}\psi_j(z_{j,P})/n_P\pmod N
\]
into \(R_i\equiv u_i/v_i\) proves (1). This argument allows different atoms of the same row to use different fields. ∎

For a binary row selection \(s\), define the **integer** sums
\[
e_{j,P}(s)=\sum_{i:s_i=1}e_{i,j,P},\qquad
E_j(s)=\sum_Pe_{j,P}(s).
\]
They must be recomputed after taking XORs of selections; adding their integer exponent vectors is generally incorrect.

### Lemma 2 — The obstruction map and its correct dimension

Let \(K_{\mathrm{pre}}\) be the left kernel before character columns are added. For each field let \(S_j\) be the primes of \(\mathcal O_{K_j}\) above rational primes dividing \(\mathcal D_j\), and put
\[
V_j=\{x\in K_j^\times:
          v_{\mathfrak p}(x)\text{ is even whenever }\mathfrak p\notin S_j\},
\qquad
H_j=V_j/K_j^{\times2}.
\]
For \(s\in K_{\mathrm{pre}}\), set
\[
\theta_j(s)=\prod_P(a_P-b_P\alpha_j)^{e_{j,P}(s)}.
\]
Then
\[
\phi_j:K_{\mathrm{pre}}\longrightarrow H_j,\qquad
s\longmapsto[\theta_j(s)]
\]
is an \(\mathbb F_2\)-linear map.

After adding the characters, let \(K\) be the resulting relation kernel and
\[
H_{j,0}=\bigcap_\chi\ker(\chi:H_j\to\{\pm1\}).
\]
The exact number of independent remaining obstructions is
\[
r_{\mathrm{obs}}
 =\dim_{\mathbb F_2}\operatorname{im}
   \left(K\longrightarrow\bigoplus_jH_{j,0}\right). \tag{2}
\]
In particular,
\[
r_{\mathrm{obs}}\le\sum_j\dim_{\mathbb F_2}H_{j,0}.
\]
If every \(|H_{j,0}|\le2\), then \(r_{\mathrm{obs}}\le J\).

**Proof.** At \(r\nmid\mathcal D_j\), Dedekind–Kummer applies to the order localized at \(r\). A primitive linear element \(a-b\alpha_j\) can have a nonzero valuation only at the degree-one prime corresponding to \(a/b\pmod r\); its valuation equals \(v_r(F_j(P))\). If \(r\mid b\), it is a unit at every prime over \(r\). Thus the ideal columns enforce even valuations outside \(S_j\).

For two selections \(s,s'\),
\[
\theta_j(s+s')
=\theta_j(s)\theta_j(s')
 \left(\prod_{i:s_i=s_i'=1}\prod_P
       (a_P-b_P\alpha_j)^{e_{i,j,P}}\right)^{-2}.
\]
Therefore square classes add linearly. Character columns restrict their images to \(H_{j,0}\), proving (2).

When \(|H_{j,0}|=2\), its identification with \(\mathbb F_2\), sending the trivial class to zero, is unique. Consequently
\[
b_j(s):=[\,\theta_j(s)\notin K_j^{\times2}\,]
\]
is linear on \(K\). When \(H_{j,0}\) is larger, this square/nonsquare indicator need not be linear.

The same exceptional quadratic field can occur in several \(K_j(\sqrt{x})\). That does not identify the \(J\) coordinate functionals on the relation kernel. ∎

### Lemma 3 — Simultaneous character filtering

There are computable bounds \(H_{j,\mathrm{bd}}\ge |H_j|\) with
\[
\log H_{j,\mathrm{bd}}=O(d\log N).
\]
For each field, take independently
\[
t_{\chi,j}
=\left\lceil
5d\bigl(\log H_{j,\mathrm{bd}}
       +2\log\log N+\log(4J)\bigr)
\right\rceil. \tag{3}
\]
With the character-prime size described below,
\[
\Pr[\exists j:\ |H_{j,0}|>2]
 \le \frac1{4(\log N)^2}<\frac14. \tag{4}
\]

**Proof.** The standard valuation–unit–class-group sequence gives
\[
|H_j|\le 2^{|S_j|+d}h_{K_j}
       \le2^{d\omega(\mathcal D_j)+d}h_{K_j}.
\]
The discriminant and elementary ideal-counting bounds in [pp], Lemma H, give the asserted computable logarithmic bound.

For completeness, the reason only one class per odd-degree field can escape the character-detection estimate is as follows. For nontrivial \(h\in H_j\), the extension
\[
L_h=K_j(\sqrt h)
\]
has degree \(2d\) and bounded discriminant \(\overline\Delta_j\). Stark's exceptional-zero result, at the factorial threshold, puts any sufficiently close real zero in a quadratic subfield \(F_h\). Since \(d\) is odd, \(K_j\) has no quadratic subfield and \(L_h\) has at most one. Thus \(L_h=K_jF_h\). Distinct square classes give distinct \(F_h\), whereas Landau's exceptional-zero uniqueness permits at most one such quadratic field in the required zero-free region.

For every other class, effective Chebotarev applied to \(L_h/K_j\), followed by removal of prime ideals of degree at least two, gives
\[
\Pr_{\mathfrak p}[\chi_{\mathfrak p}(h)=-1]\ge\frac1{5d}.
\]
The sampler choosing a rational prime \(r\), then accepting its roots with total probability \(\#Z_r/d\), is uniform on degree-one prime ideals in its interval.

Hence the probability that some nonexceptional class of field \(j\) survives is at most
\[
|H_j|\exp(-t_{\chi,j}/(5d))
 \le \frac1{4J(\log N)^2}.
\]
A union bound proves (4). A surviving subgroup contained in the identity plus one exceptional class has order at most two. ∎

Use character primes with
\[
s_\chi=
\left\lceil(2d)!\,
 \bigl(\log N+\max_j\log\overline\Delta_j+d\bigr)^4\right\rceil
\]
bits, increasing a fixed numerical multiplier if necessary. This makes both the Chebotarev error and the nonexceptional real-zero term negligible. In particular,
\[
s_\chi^{O(1)}
=\exp(O(d\log d))(\log N)^{O(1)}
=\exp(o(\Lambda)). \tag{5}
\]

This is a per-character bit cost, not a claim that the character primes themselves have polynomial size.

### Lemma 4 — An exact square-membership oracle

For a selection \(s\in K\), put
\[
\gamma^-_{j,s}
 =\prod_{e_{j,P}(s)<0}z_{j,P}^{|e_{j,P}(s)|},
\]
and
\[
\Gamma_{j,s}
 =h_j'(\beta_j)^2
   \prod_Pz_{j,P}^{|e_{j,P}(s)|}
 \in\mathbb Z[\beta_j]. \tag{6}
\]
Then
\[
\Gamma_{j,s}\in K_j^{\times2}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\theta_j(s)\in K_j^{\times2}. \tag{7}
\]
There is a Las Vegas algorithm that either returns an exactly verified
\(\eta_j^2=\Gamma_{j,s}\), or correctly reports NONSQUARE.

**Proof.** The parity column makes \(E_j(s)\) even, and
\[
\prod_Pz_{j,P}^{e_{j,P}(s)}
=t_j^{E_j(s)}\theta_j(s).
\]
Changing signed exponents to absolute exponents multiplies this by
\((\gamma^-_{j,s})^2\). This proves (7).

Here is the complete square test. Write \(A_j=\mathbb Z[\beta_j]\). Choose a prime
\[
p_j\in(B,2B],\qquad p_j\nmid\mathcal D_j,
\]
and factor \(h_j\bmod p_j\). All components of \(\Gamma_{j,s}\) are units: its norm has prime divisors only at primes at most \(B\) or dividing \(\mathcal D_j\).

Test squareness in each residue field. If a component is nonsquare, return NONSQUARE. Otherwise enumerate the \(2^{\tau_j-1}\) local sign choices, where \(\tau_j\le d\) is the number of irreducible factors of \(h_j\bmod p_j\). For each choice, lift an inverse square root by
\[
z\longmapsto z+z(1-\Gamma_{j,s}z^2)/2,
\]
and reconstruct \(\eta=\Gamma_{j,s}z\) with centered coefficients.

The following explicit coefficient bound makes the test complete. Set
\[
M_h=\lfloor\|h_j\|_2\rfloor+1,\qquad
h_* =d\|h_j\|_1M_h^{d-1},
\]
\[
U=h_*^2\prod_P
   \bigl(\|z_{j,P}\|_1M_h^{d-1}\bigr)^{|e_{j,P}(s)|},
\]
\[
A_{\mathrm{sq}}
=d\lceil\sqrt U\rceil\,2^{d-1}M_hh_*^{d-1}. \tag{8}
\]
Lift until \(p_j^e>2A_{\mathrm{sq}}\), and test \(\eta^2=\Gamma_{j,s}\) exactly.

If \(\Gamma_{j,s}=\delta^2\) in \(K_j\), then
\[
(\delta/h_j'(\beta_j))^2\in A_j,
\]
so \(\delta/h_j'(\beta_j)\in\mathcal O_{K_j}\). The trace-dual identity
\[
A_j^\vee=h_j'(\beta_j)^{-1}A_j
\]
implies \(h_j'(\beta_j)\mathcal O_{K_j}\subset A_j\), hence \(\delta\in A_j\).

Every conjugate of \(\delta\) has absolute value at most \(\sqrt U\). Inverting the Vandermonde matrix gives (8): its numerators are bounded by \(2^{d-1}M_h\), and
\[
\prod_\sigma |h_j'(\sigma\beta_j)|
=|\operatorname{disc}(h_j)|\ge1
\]
bounds each denominator from below by \(h_*^{-(d-1)}\). One enumerated local sign choice therefore reconstructs \(\pm\delta\). If none passes exact verification, the element is nonsquare. ∎

No factorization of the field discriminant or computation of its class group is required.

### Lemma 5 — Elimination of all remaining obstructions

Let \(C_{\mathrm{MF}}\) be the number of columns specified in Lemma 1, including (3). Collect
\[
M_{\mathrm{rel}}\ge C_{\mathrm{MF}}+J+12 \tag{9}
\]
rows. There is a Las Vegas algorithm returning a nonempty selection \(S\) such that every \(\theta_j(S)\) is a square, together with exact square-root certificates.

**Algorithm.**

1. Sample the characters and construct the matrix.
2. Compute \(J+1\) linearly independent vectors \(s_1,\dots,s_{J+1}\) of its left kernel.
3. Using Lemma 4, compute
   \[
   b_{ji}=[\,\Gamma_{j,s_i}\text{ is nonsquare}\,].
   \]
4. Find a nonzero
   \[
   x\in\ker\bigl((b_{ji})_{j,i}\bigr),
   \qquad S=\sum_i x_is_i.
   \]
5. Recompute all integer exponents for \(S\), and run Lemma 4 in every field. Return only if all square roots verify exactly; otherwise restart with fresh characters.

**Proof.** By (9), the relation kernel has dimension at least \(J+12\), so step 2 is always possible. It can be performed deterministically by Gaussian elimination; uniform kernel sampling is unnecessary.

On the event of Lemma 3, every \(b_j\) is linear. Therefore step 4 annihilates every \(\phi_j\). Independence of the \(s_i\) ensures \(S\ne0\). Lemma 4 then succeeds in every field.

If character filtering failed, step 5 prevents an incorrect output. A fresh character set is good with probability at least \(3/4\), for every fixed collection of rows and fields. Thus the expected number of repetitions is at most \(4/3\). ∎

This replaces an exponential search through \(2^J\) combinations by a \(J\times(J+1)\) binary system.

### Lemma 6 — Reconstruction of the congruence

For the selection \(S\) returned by Lemma 5, define
\[
Q_S=g_1^{\frac12\sum_jE_j(S)}
       \prod_{p\le B}
       p^{-\frac12\sum_{j,P}e_{j,P}(S)v_p(n_P)}.
\]
The rational sign column makes \(\prod_{i\in S}q_i\) positive, and the other rational columns give
\[
Q_S^2=\prod_{i\in S}q_i.
\]
Put
\[
Y_S=
Q_S\prod_j
\left[
t_j^{-E_j(S)/2}
\frac{\psi_j(\eta_j)}
{\psi_j(\gamma^-_{j,S})\,\psi_j(h_j'(\beta_j))}
\right]\pmod N. \tag{10}
\]
Then
\[
Y_S^2\equiv\prod_{i\in S}R_i\pmod N. \tag{11}
\]

**Proof.** From (6),
\[
\frac{\psi_j(\eta_j)^2}
     {\psi_j(\gamma^-_{j,S})^2\psi_j(h_j'(\beta_j))^2}
=\prod_P\psi_j(z_{j,P})^{e_{j,P}(S)}.
\]
Multiplying these identities and using (1) proves (11).

Every inverse in (10) exists. Indeed,
\[
\psi_j(z_{j,P})=t_jG(P)/g_1
\]
is a unit, and
\[
\psi_j(h_j'(\beta_j))
=t_j^{d-2}f_j'(m)
\]
is a unit by the field check. The rational factors involve only \(g_1\) and primes at most \(B\). ∎

Finally set \(X_S=\prod_{i\in S}r_i\pmod N\). Exact verification of \(X_S^2\equiv Y_S^2\pmod N\) is inexpensive.

### Lemma 7 — Transcript invariance and fruitfulness

Suppose:

- each \(r_i\) is uniform on \([1,N-1]\);
- field generation, recursive factorization, atom selection, characters, linear algebra and square-root algorithms use coins independent of the hidden roots;
- these procedures receive \(R_i=r_i^2\), not \(r_i\);
- after a trivial final gcd, all rows are discarded before another fruitfulness trial.

Then \(S,Y_S\) are measurable functions of the squares \(R_i\) and independent coins. If \(\omega(N)\ge2\),
\[
\Pr[1<\gcd(X_S-Y_S,N)<N]\ge\frac12.
\]

**Proof.** All operations in Lemmas 1–6 use certificates derived from \(R_i\) and independent coins. This includes choosing the field of an atom, exact square/nonsquare decisions, and choosing \(S\). Recursive shape tests return the canonical prime factorization of inputs determined by \(R_i\); fresh recursion coins preserve the assertion.

Conditional on all \(R_i\) and these coins, each unit root has the form
\[
r_i=\epsilon_i\rho(R_i),
\]
where the \(\epsilon_i\) are independent uniform elements of
\[
\mu_2(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z),\qquad |\mu_2|=2^{\omega(N)}.
\]
Since \(S\ne\varnothing\), \(X_S/Y_S\) is uniform on this group. Only its two elements \(\pm1\) give a trivial gcd. The success probability is
\[
1-2^{1-\omega(N)}\ge\frac12.
\]

Character retries may reuse the rows: their outcomes are transcript-measurable. A previous **final gcd** is different; it reveals information about hidden root signs. Fresh rows are required after a trivial final gcd. ∎

### Costs and an explicit MF constant

For the parameters in the question, write
\[
\theta_0=
\begin{cases}
3/d_0,&\kappa\ge d_0^2/4,\\[2mm]
2/d_0+d_0/(4\kappa),&\kappa<d_0^2/4.
\end{cases}
\]
Since \(\mu D^{1/5}\to1/d_0\), the rational and algebraic smoothness exponents are
\[
\sigma_r=\frac{2}{5ad_0},\qquad
\sigma_F=\frac{2}{5a}\left(\theta_0-\frac1{d_0}\right),\qquad
\sigma=\sigma_r+\sigma_F. \tag{12}
\]
Thus
\[
\rho_r=e^{-(\sigma_r+o(1))\Lambda},\qquad
\rho_F=e^{-(\sigma_F+o(1))\Lambda},
\qquad J=e^{(\sigma_F+o(1))\Lambda}.
\]

Use the rational-only box promised in §11:
\[
S_r^2\rho_r=(\log N)^{O(1)},\qquad
\log S_r^2=(\sigma_r+o(1))\Lambda.
\]
A Rankin scan, as in Lemma CS, supplies a computable choice with a polylogarithmic lower bound on \(S_r^2\rho_r\). Using a sufficiently large fixed polylogarithmic multiplier and the Rankin approximation also makes \(J\) computable.

The columns and rows satisfy
\[
C_{\mathrm{MF}}
\le1+\pi(B)+J\bigl(1+d\pi(B)+\max_jt_{\chi,j}\bigr),
\]
\[
M_{\mathrm{rel}}=e^{(b+o(1))\Lambda},
\qquad b:=a+\sigma_F. \tag{13}
\]

Here is a conservative ledger. It charges a worst-case special-\(q\) search uniformly over retained bundles, avoiding assumptions about the number of rationally smooth candidates in a biased retained bundle.

| Operation | Cost or size | \(L[2/5]\)-exponent |
|---|---:|---:|
| Sieve to \(B\), Rankin scan | \(B(\log N)^{O(1)}\) | \(a\) |
| Generate and certify \(J\) raw fields | \(J(\log N)^{O(1)}\) | \(\sigma_F\) |
| Factor-base roots, all fields | \(J B\,\operatorname{poly}(d,\log N)\) | \(b\) |
| Total characters | \(J\,\operatorname{poly}(d,\log N,\log J)\) | \(\sigma_F\) |
| Character-prime bit operations | preceding quantity times \(\exp(O(d\log d))\) | \(\sigma_F\) |
| Rows and sparse matrix entries | \(M_{\mathrm{rel}}(\log N)^{O(1)}\) | \(b\) |
| Rational enumeration per special \(q\) | \(O(S_r^2)\) values | \(\sigma_r\) |
| All algebraic values per special \(q\), worst case | \(O(JS_r^2)\) values | \(\sigma\) |
| Batch smoothness and chosen-value factorization | \(\widetilde O(B+JS_r^2)\operatorname{polylog}N\) | \(\max(a,\sigma)\) |
| Hensel-prime setup, all fields | at most \(\widetilde O(JB)\) | \(b\) |
| One exact SQ call | \(M_{\mathrm{rel}}\exp(o(\Lambda))\), conservatively | \(b\) |
| At most \(J(J+2)\) SQ calls | \(J^2M_{\mathrm{rel}}\exp(o(\Lambda))\) | \(a+3\sigma_F\) |
| Obstruction-bit elimination | \(O(J^3)\) | \(3\sigma_F\) |
| Deterministic full kernel basis | \(O(M_{\mathrm{rel}}^3)\) | \(3b\) |
| Alternatively, \(J+1\) sparse kernel samples | \((J+1)M_{\mathrm{rel}}^2\exp(o(\Lambda))\) | \(2a+3\sigma_F\) |

The SQ bound uses
\[
\log A_{\mathrm{sq}}
\le \operatorname{poly}(d,\log N)
       \left(1+\sum_P|e_{j,P}|\right),
\]
and \(2^d=\exp(o(\Lambda))\). Its aggregate cost can be sharpened by summing atom multiplicities across fields; the displayed conservative bound already suffices.

**Height ledger.** The additional floor contributes
\[
\Delta\log H=13(\sigma+1)d\Lambda\lambda
           =O(D^{3/5}\lambda^2)
           =o(D^{4/5}\lambda).
\]
It increases \(\log X_F\) by the same amount and changes
\[
\log(1/\rho_F)
\quad\text{by}\quad
O(d\lambda^2)=o(\Lambda).
\]
Coefficient arithmetic and finite-field irreducibility certification remain polynomial in \(\log N,d\). Thus the floor changes no leading exponent in the table.

Suppose the established supply is
\[
\gamma_{\mathrm{MF}}\ge(\log N)^{-C_\gamma}.
\]
The split-counting and size-biased bundle-retention argument give
\[
p^{-1}=e^{(s+o(1))\Lambda},
\qquad s=2\kappa(1+C_\gamma).
\]
The argument depends on a fixed bundle and its row probability; it does not require a single field.

For the deterministic Gaussian-elimination implementation just proved, a valid constant is
\[
\boxed{
c_{\mathrm{MF,dense}}
=
\max\left\{
3(a+\sigma_F),\
a+\sigma_F+s+\max(a,\sigma),\
\frac{a+\sigma_F+s}{1-2^{-2/5}}
\right\}. }\tag{14}
\]
Using the sparse kernel sampler with its usual verified solve bound gives
\[
\boxed{
c_{\mathrm{MF,sparse}}
=
\max\left\{
2a+3\sigma_F,\
a+\sigma_F+s+\max(a,\sigma),\
\frac{a+\sigma_F+s}{1-2^{-2/5}}
\right\}. }\tag{15}
\]

Indeed, the recursion has the form
\[
T(N)\le
L_N[2/5,\max(P_{\rm post},b+s+\max(a,\sigma))+o(1)]
+
L_N[2/5,b+s+o(1)]\,T(\sqrt{2N}),
\]
and scaling the recursive argument multiplies its exponent by \(2^{-2/5}+o(1)\). Alternation with trial division ensures finite running time below all analytic thresholds.

If §1's original box is retained, so \(\log S^2\sim\sigma\Lambda\), replace the search exponent \(\max(a,\sigma)\) by \(\max(a,\sigma+\sigma_F)\).

**Therefore \(J=(\log N)^C/\rho_F\) is affordable, but the row target and constant grow substantially.** The bound \((JdB)^2\) in the outline does not, by itself, account for producing \(J+1\) independent kernel vectors.

## 2. Probability audit and failure modes

### Accepted-field mass

Fix \(g,\ell,P_0,w\), with \(P_0\) selected using rational data alone. Write
\[
p_1=\rho_F(\log N)^{-C_g}.
\]
A raw lower bound \(\Pr_c[F(P_0)\text{ smooth}]\ge p_1\) implies an accepted-field lower bound only after bounding
\[
\Pr_c[\text{rejected}\mid g,w].
\]

For the prime-multiplier law, the supplied proofs give, uniformly in fixed \(g,w\),
\[
\varepsilon_{\rm rej}
\le
\exp\!\left(-\frac{\log H}{13d\log\log H}\right)
+\frac3{2H+1}+\frac1W.
\]
Since \(H<N\), the extra floor gives
\[
\frac{\log H}{13d\log\log H}\ge(\sigma+1)\Lambda.
\]
Consequently
\[
\varepsilon_{\rm rej}=o(p_1).
\]
Thus
\[
\Pr[\text{smooth at }P_0
       \text{ and accepted, or a factor exit}\mid g,\ell,w]
\ge p_1/2.
\]

The old rejection estimate without the floor is inadequate: an event of probability \(e^{-\Theta(D^{2/5})}\) can contain all the mass \(p_1=e^{-\Theta(D^{2/5}\lambda)}\). A bound such as \(2^{-d}\) is still less useful here.

Content \(w\) is a special case: because \(w>B\), a nonzero raw \(B\)-smooth value cannot occur in that rejection event. Nevertheless, the uniform subtraction bound above is sufficient.

For \(J\ge4/p_1\), conditional independence of the raw draws gives
\[
\Pr[\text{no accepted success at }P_0\mid g,\ell]\le e^{-2}.
\]
No independence between the fields' **accepted success indicators and their rejection events** is asserted or needed.

### Rational supply

The rational supply follows from Proposition R after making its common-box geometry explicit. It does not follow merely from the rational first moment.

Let \(N_r\) count filtered rationally smooth candidates and put \(A=S_r^2\rho_r\). Uniformly at growing degree,
\[
\mathbb E N_r\ge A(\log N)^{-A_1},\qquad
\mathbb E[N_r(N_r-1)]
 \le A^2(\log N)^{A_2}. \tag{16}
\]

Here is the essential second-moment check. For a fixed pair \(P,P'\), write
\[
|\det(P,P')|=\ell q,\qquad 1\le q\le4S_r^2.
\]
The image of the \(g\)-box under
\[
g\longmapsto(G(P)/\ell,G(P')/\ell)
\]
lies in a lattice of index \(q\), inside a parallelogram coverable by
\(O(qS_r^2)\) squares of side \(M/V\). Proposition R's smooth-progression bound gives at most
\[
\frac{(M/V)^2}{q}\rho_r^2(\log N)^C
\]
smooth lattice points per square. Thus the pair admits at most
\[
\frac{M^2}{\ell}\rho_r^2(\log N)^C
\]
rational sides. Each \(P\) has \(O(S_r^2)\) partners. Summation and division by \(|\mathcal G|\asymp M^2\) prove (16).

The progression bound used here is elementary divisor switching plus local smooth-density comparisons. For an interval of length \(A'\), modulus \(q\le B^{C_0}\), first remove \(\gcd(r,q)\); then every smooth remaining integer has a smooth divisor
\[
D'/B<d'\le D',\qquad
D'=\min(A'/q,X/(eB^{C_0})).
\]
Counting its complementary factor gives
\[
\frac{2A'}q\sum_{\substack{D'/B<d'\le D'\\P^+(d')\le B}}\frac1{d'}.
\]
Since \(D'\ge XB^{-2C_0}\), local density comparisons bound this by
\[
(A'/q)\rho(X)(\log N)^{O(C_0)}.
\]
All losses are polylogarithmic because \(C_0\) is fixed. The narrow \(b\)-window costs a polynomial factor in \(d\), also polylogarithmic.

For the prime-multiplier design, excluding bad \(t=|G(P)|\) requires Lemma 8.5′ and its rational bad-mass argument. Given that input,
\[
\mathbb E N_r^{\rm good}\ge \tfrac12\mathbb E N_r.
\]
If \(A\ge(\log N)^{-C_0}\), Paley–Zygmund yields
\[
\gamma_r
\ge
\frac1{(\log N)^{2A_1+A_2}
       +(\log N)^{A_1+C_0}},
\]
after absorbing fixed constants. One may take
\[
C_\gamma=
1+\max(2A_1+A_2,A_1+C_0)
\]
and enlarge it for the fixed amplification factors.

Thus the rational argument is repairable and sufficient. Its “good modulus” qualification remains an analytic dependency.

| Severity | Failure mode | Finding and fix |
|---|---|---|
| **FATAL to the inference without repair** | Rejected fields absorb raw smoothness mass | Small unconditional rejection probability is insufficient. Use the stated extra height floor and a bound uniform in fixed \(g,w\). |
| **MAJOR** | \(P_0\) is selected using accepted fields or algebraic successes | Then the advertised conditioning is circular. Choose the first rationally eligible good \(P\) using only \(g,\ell\). |
| **MAJOR** | Independence asserted after arbitrary joint conditioning | Work with independent raw draws and subtract rejection inside each draw. Rejected slots can be omitted. |
| **MAJOR** | Single-field row target retained | Use \(C_{\rm MF}+J+12\), not \(C_{\rm single}+12\). |
| **MAJOR** | Square/nonsquare treated as linear unconditionally | It is linear only when the surviving group has order at most two. Final exact verification is mandatory. |
| **MAJOR** | Character failure probability bounded per field only | Add \(\log J\) in (3) and take a union bound. |
| **MAJOR** | Kernel production charged once | Dense elimination costs \(M_{\rm rel}^3\); the stated sparse sampler used \(J+1\) times costs \((J+1)M_{\rm rel}^2\). |
| **MAJOR** | Expected rational-candidate count used after field retention | Retention biases the bundle. Use the uniform \(JS_r^2\) bound unless an appropriate weighted expectation is proved. |
| **MAJOR** | Rational supply inferred from its first moment | Supply needs Proposition R's second moment and a lower bound on \(S_r^2\rho_r\). |
| **MAJOR** | Reusing rows after a trivial final gcd | This conditions hidden root signs. Discard those rows. |
| **MAJOR** | Replacing prime \(w\) by integer \(w\) but retaining the old content-rejection rule | That discards useful mass. Normalize by content, as below. |
| **MINOR** | One common Hensel prime required to avoid every discriminant | Unnecessary and potentially costly. Choose a suitable prime separately in each field. |
| **MINOR** | Certification over \(J\) fields omitted | It costs \(J\operatorname{polylog}N\); factor-base setup costs \(JB\operatorname{polylog}N\). Both are in the ledger. |
| **MINOR** | XOR selections implemented by adding integer exponents | Recompute the selected-row integer sums before square-root reconstruction. |

## 3. The analytic input and ways around it

### Where Drappeau's Lemme 5 enters

Put
\[
\mathcal H(u)=\exp\{u/\log^2(u+1)\}.
\]
The input used in Lemma 8.5′ is a small-conductor estimate of the form
\[
\sum_{q\le Q}\frac1{\varphi(q)}
 \sum_{\substack{\chi\bmod q\\1<\operatorname{cond}\chi\le x^\eta}}
 |\Psi(x,y;\chi)|
\ll_A
\Psi(x,y)\{\mathcal H(u)^{-\delta}(\log x)^{-A}+y^{-\delta}\}. \tag{17}
\]

In Lemma 8.5′ it enters the **small-conductor part for smooth moduli**. Möbius removal of imprimitive coprimality conditions and averaging over smooth multiples extract the density of the smooth modulus family. The large-conductor terms, including those whose conductor contains \(\ell\), are handled separately by the large sieve.

The resulting restricted estimate is
\[
\sum_{t\in\mathcal T_Q}\max_{(a,t)=1}|E(x,y;t,a)|
\ll
\Psi(x,y)\frac{\rho(Q/Y,y)}{\log Y}\,\varepsilon. \tag{18}
\]
The density factor is essential. Markov's inequality then makes most \(t=\ell n\) good **relative to this sparse family**. GM-U uses their progression lower bounds, which enter the accepted-field amplification.

I verified the statement and provenance paragraph of **Drappeau, Compositio Mathematica 151 (2015), 828–862, Lemme 5**, in the author/arXiv text. Its proof is referred to Harper §3.3. Harper's **arXiv:1208.5992, §3.3, equation (3.1)** explicitly supplies the effective estimate without the extra logarithmic saving; that weaker effective version is sufficient for this application. I checked that display and its surrounding argument, but did not independently reprove every zero-density and exceptional-character estimate. [Drappeau, Lemme 5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.7554), [Harper, §3.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992).

A published lemma may legitimately be cited as a theorem. Its dependence on an unpublished proof does not disprove it. It does prevent describing the resulting argument as self-contained without reproducing or independently checking that proof.

### A field-law replacement avoiding progression equidistribution

There is a concrete alternative to (17)–(18).

**Integer-multiplier law.** Draw
\[
w\ \text{uniformly from }\{W+1,\dots,2W\},
\]
without requiring primality. Construct the same base expansion and raw polynomial
\[
\widetilde F=\widehat F_{g,w}+GR_c.
\]
Gcd-check \(w\), the nonzero leading coefficient and the content against \(N\). A proper gcd is a successful factor exit. Replace the polynomial by its primitive part, rather than rejecting nontrivial content. Require a degree-\(d\) irreducibility certificate and the derivative-unit check.

The base-expansion construction and its coefficient bounds remain valid for integer \(w\). The prime hypothesis was needed for some rejection and residue arguments, not for constructing the expansion.

#### Lemma 8 — Pointwise smoothness by integer-multiplier averaging

Fix any filtered rationally eligible \(g,\ell,P\), with \(\ell\nmid N\), and put \(t=|G(P)|\). Assume the same coefficient-smoothing and size margins as GM-U, and \(W/t\to\infty\). Then
\[
\Pr_{w,c}[\widetilde F(P)\ne0
               \text{ and }\widetilde F(P)\text{ is }B\text{-smooth}]
\ge \rho_F(\log N)^{-C}. \tag{19}
\]
No good-modulus hypothesis is needed.

**Proof.** Rational eligibility implies
\[
\gcd(t,Nbg_1)=1.
\]
Homogeneity gives
\[
\widehat F_{g,w}(P)\equiv c_Pw\pmod t,
\qquad c_P=Nb^dg_1^{-d}\in(\mathbb Z/t\mathbb Z)^\times. \tag{20}
\]

Apply one-point smoothing to \(Z=\langle c,v(P)\rangle\). Its comparison law is independent of \(w\) and is a mixture of uniform integer intervals centered at \(M_0\). Apart from mass \(O(1/\varpi)\), their radii \(y\) satisfy
\[
y\ge(2H+1)V'^{d-1}/\varpi,\qquad |M_0|\le y/2.
\]
Put \(y'=\lfloor y/2\rfloor\). Choose the sign of the half-interval so that \(GZ\) increases positively.

There is a bound \(A\), uniform in \(w\), such that
\[
|\widehat F_{g,w}(P)|\le A,\qquad A\ll ty'/4.
\]
Choose a common \(x\in[ty'/4,ty'/2]\), independent of \(w\). The values in the layer include every integer
\[
A<n\le x,\qquad n\equiv c_Pw\pmod t.
\]
Therefore their smooth count is at least
\[
\Psi(x,B;t,c_Pw)-(A/t+1). \tag{21}
\]

Every residue class modulo \(t\) occurs at least \(\lfloor W/t\rfloor\) times among the \(w\)'s. Since multiplication by \(c_P\) permutes all residue classes,
\[
\frac1W\sum_{w=W+1}^{2W}\Psi(x,B;t,c_Pw)
\ge
\frac{\lfloor W/t\rfloor}{W}\Psi(x,B)
\ge
(1-t/W)\frac{\Psi(x,B)}t. \tag{22}
\]
This is an exact finite counting inequality; it uses no smooth-number distribution theorem.

Divide (21)–(22) by the layer length \(O(y)\). Since \(x/t\asymp y\), and \(x\) differs from \(X_F\) by at most a fixed power of \(B\), the local smooth-density estimates give
\[
\Pr[\text{smooth}\mid\text{layer}]
\ge
\rho_F(\log N)^{-C}
-O((A/t+1)/y).
\]
The latter error, the small-layer loss and the total-variation error are absorbed by the existing \(N^{\eta_0}\) margins. This proves (19). ∎

#### Lemma 9 — Rejections for the integer-multiplier law

Uniformly in fixed admissible \(g\), for large \(N\),
\[
\Pr_{w,c}[\text{rejected without a factor exit}]
\le
\exp\!\left(-\frac{\log H}{30d\log\log H}\right)
+O(H^{-1}). \tag{23}
\]
Thus the sufficient replacement height floor
\[
\log H\ge30(\sigma+1)d\Lambda\lambda \tag{24}
\]
preserves at least half the mass in (19).

**Proof.** Let
\[
Q_{\rm IR}=\prod_{16<r\le\frac12\log H}r=H^{1/2+o(1)}.
\]
The stated parameters satisfy \(Q_{\rm IR}/W=o(1)\) and
\(dQ_{\rm IR}/H=o(1)\).

For fixed \(w\) and a certificate prime \(r\nmid w\), the affine coefficient family modulo \(r\) has irreducible fraction at least \(1/(4d)\), exactly as in Lemma IR. This remains true when \(r\mid g_1\): the prescribed leading coefficient is then nonzero because \(r\nmid Nw\).

CRT independence for uniform \(c\bmod Q_{\rm IR}\), and its bounded comparison with the coefficient box, give
\[
\Pr_c[\text{no raw certificate}\mid w]
\le e\prod_{\substack{r\mid Q_{\rm IR}\\r\nmid w}}
                (1-1/(4d)).
\]
Averaging the nearly uniform residues of integer \(w\bmod Q_{\rm IR}\) bounds this by
\[
2e\prod_{r\mid Q_{\rm IR}}
 \left(1-\frac{1-1/r}{4d}\right)
\le
\exp\!\left(-\frac{\log H}{30d\log\log H}\right).
\]
The deliberately loose constant \(30\) absorbs the comparison factors.

A zero leading coefficient and \(N\mid\widetilde f'(m)\) each have probability \(O(H^{-1})\), by fixing the other coefficients. Proper nonunit gcds are factor exits.

Finally, let \(e\) be the content. If its gcd with \(N\) passes, \(e\) is invertible modulo \(N\); moreover \(e\mid Nw\) implies \(e\mid w\). The primitive part still vanishes at \(m\). A nonzero smooth raw value remains smooth after division by \(e\). A raw irreducibility certificate certifies the primitive part as well. Thus content normalization loses no mass used in (19). ∎

Both (19) and (23) are conditional on fixed \(g,P\), which is the conditioning required by MF. Independent field draws remain independent after taking their primitive parts.

Consequently this variant needs only the **unqualified** rational supply in (16). It removes Lemma 8.5′, GM-U's good-modulus condition, and the associated bad-mass argument. Its leading costs remain (12)–(15); replacing \(13\) by \(30\) changes no leading exponent.

These lemmas constitute a new argument in this answer, not a published result. They need independent checking, particularly the common-layer interval and the joint CRT rejection estimate.

### Published alternatives checked

“Verified” below means that I inspected the relevant statement in a primary text or author copy. It does not mean that I re-proved the whole paper.

| Source and exact result | What it supplies | Does it replace the needed input? Verification |
|---|---|---|
| **Fouvry–Tenenbaum**, *Proc. London Math. Soc.* (3) **63** (1991), 449–494, **Théorème 6**, (1.30) | BV with an absolute \(x(\log x)^{-A}\) error, uniformly in the smoothness bound, at essentially square-root level. The text notes that §7 proves a slightly weaker variant of the stated result. | **No.** The absolute error overwhelms the sparse smooth-number main term here. Statement/provenance checked in the author-uploaded scan; the formula's OCR is imperfect. [Author copy](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/243090669_Entiers_Sans_Grand_Facteur_Premier_En_Progressions_Arithmetiques). |
| **Fouvry–Tenenbaum**, *Proc. London Math. Soc.* (3) **72** (1996), 481–514, **Théorème 1 and Corollaire 1** | For \(y>\exp((\log x)^{2/3+\varepsilon})\), Corollary 1 gives a \(\Psi(x,y)\mathcal H(u)^{-c}(\log x)^{-A}\) error up to \(Q=\sqrt x\,e^{-(\log x)^{1/3}}\). | **No in the present range.** Here \(y=L_x[1/2,\Theta(1)]\). Checked in the author's corrected copy, which explicitly differs from the printed version. [Author copy](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/286726767_Repartition_statistique_des_entiers_sans_grand_facteur_premier_dans_les_progressions_arithmetiques). |
| **Granville**, *Acta Math.* **170** (1993), 255–273, **Theorem 2**, (1.6)–(1.7) | A \(\Psi(x,y)/\log^A y\) BV bound, with \(Q\le\exp(C\log y\log\log y/\log\log\log y)\) and \(x\ge Q^2\log^B Q\). | **No.** At \(y=L_x[1/2]\), this permits only \(Q=x^{o(1)}\), whereas the relevant moduli are a fixed positive power of \(x\). Numbered statement checked in the indexed primary PDF text. [Published paper](https://archive.ymsc.tsinghua.edu.cn/pacm_download/117/6484-11511_2006_Article_BF02392787.pdf). |
| **Granville**, same paper, **Theorems 1 and 3**; also *Philos. Trans. R. Soc. A* **345** (1993), 349–362, main result | Individual progression comparisons for moduli bounded by suitable fixed powers of \(y\); the second paper gives asymptotic equidistribution for \(q\le y^{1-\varepsilon}\) when \(\log x/\log q\to\infty\). | **No.** Here \(\log t/\log B\to\infty\). The first paper's numbered statements were checked; the second paper's theorem numbering was **not verified**, owing to an unreadable text layer. [Author's publication descriptions](https://intra.dms.umontreal.ca/~andrew/1993.php). |
| **Harper**, *J. Number Theory* **132** (2012), 182–199, **Theorems 1–2** | Theorem 1 gives individual equidistribution for \(q\le y^{4\sqrt e-\delta}\), as \(\log x/\log q\to\infty\); Theorem 2 gives a bounded-index coset statement for \(q\le y^A\). | **No.** Neither reaches \(t=y^{\Theta(u)}\). Both statements verified. [Author preprint](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.2106). |
| **Harper**, **arXiv:1208.5992 (2012), Theorem 1; §3.3, (3.1)** | BV for \((\log x)^K\le y\), and the small-conductor saving underlying (17). | **Yes as the existing input**, followed by the restricted-modulus transfer. It is the unpublished dependency under examination. Relevant displays verified; complete proof not independently reproduced. [Preprint](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992). |
| **Harper**, *Compositio Math.* **152** (2016), 1121–1158, **Theorems 1–2** | Minor-arc estimates and sharp \(p>2\) moment/restriction bounds for exponential sums over smooth numbers, down to polylogarithmic \(y\). | **No direct replacement.** These are upper bounds, not the required lower bound in a prescribed large residue class or the relative sparse-modulus BV estimate. Statements checked in the accepted author version. [Author version](https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/107457/7/WRAP-minor-arcs-mean-values-restriction-theory-exponential-sums-over-smooth-numbers-Harper-2018.pdf). |
| **de la Bretèche–Drappeau**, *JEMS* **22** (2020), 1577–1624, **Théorème 2.1**, especially (2.2) | BV restricted to smooth moduli, including a maximum over residues below \(x^{1/2-\varepsilon}\), with error \(\Psi(x,y_1)\Psi(Q,y_2)Q^{-1}e^{O_k(u_2)}(\log x)^{-A}\), with the stated interval and auxiliary-modulus factors. | **Not a clean replacement.** The displayed bound need not be relatively small when \(y_1=y_2=L_x[1/2]\). Its proof explicitly returns to Harper §3.3 and (3.1). Statement and that dependency verified in the author version. [Author version](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1703.03197). |
| **Pascadi**, *Compositio Math.* **161** (2025), 1923–1974, **Theorem 1.1; Lemma 3.2** | Theorem 1.1 reaches moduli \(x^{66/107-\varepsilon}\) for a fixed nonzero residue. Lemma 3.2 reproduces the small-conductor estimate (17). | **No independent replacement.** Fixed residue is insufficient for the original forced residues; Lemma 3.2 explicitly cites Drappeau Lemme 5 and Harper §3.3. Both verified in the published text. [Published article](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/compositio-mathematica/article/smooth-numbers-in-arithmetic-progressions-to-large-moduli/D8E4EE07BDAA6D41CCD8C5455D81CD7A). |

Two qualifications matter when comparing these results.

First, an unrestricted bound
\[
\sum_{q\le Q}\max_a|E(x,y;q,a)|
\ll \Psi(x,y)(\log x)^{-A}
\]
does not automatically imply (18). The restricted family's density is subexponentially small, and fixed logarithmic savings cannot recover it.

Second, stronger levels beyond \(1/2\) for a fixed residue do not address an arbitrary forced residue \(c_Pw\bmod t\).

### What exponent follows from weaker inputs?

There are separate issues of smoothness range and precision.

If an input requires
\[
\log y\gtrsim(\log x)^\beta,
\]
write \(d=D^\delta\), so \(\log x\asymp D^{1-\delta}\lambda\). The one-level geometry and split budget require
\[
\delta\le\alpha/2,
\]
while the input range requires
\[
\delta\ge1-\alpha/\beta.
\]
A necessary exponent is therefore
\[
\alpha\ge\frac{2\beta}{2+\beta}. \tag{25}
\]
For \(\beta=2/3+\varepsilon\), this is strictly above \(1/2\), approaching \(1/2\) as \(\varepsilon\downarrow0\). This is only a necessary range calculation: it does not establish the missing relative sparse-family estimate.

Separately, if relation supply loses
\[
\log(1/\gamma)=O(d^\tau\log\log N),
\]
the stated geometry gives
\[
\alpha\ge\frac{2+\tau}{5+2\tau};
\]
thus \(\tau=1\) gives \(3/7\). A BV estimate too weak to show any relatively large good set does not automatically give \(3/7\).

Without a sufficient replacement, the established arbitrary-integer fallback remains Lenstra–Pomerance, *JAMS* **5** (1992), 483–516, the unnumbered main theorem in §1: expected \(L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]\). I checked that statement. [Published paper](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf).

## 4. Assessment

**The multi-field square-root step is realistic and can be made fully checkable.** Lemmas 1–7 give a complete implementation with exact verification and an explicit conservative constant. It requires more rows, simultaneous character filtering, and more linear algebra than the single-field construction.

For the **original prime-multiplier Design MF**, the single weakest link is the accepted-field pointwise smoothness lower bound: GM-U depends on the relative restricted BV estimate in Lemma 8.5′. MF removes the algebraic second moment; it does not remove that analytic dependency. The extra height floor correctly addresses rejection absorption.

The **integer-multiplier variant** offers a more direct route to a self-contained proof. Lemmas 8–9 replace the progression-distribution input by exact residue averaging, coefficient smoothing, and CRT counting. The rational second moment then uses only divisor switching and local smooth-density estimates. This is a concrete mathematical modification, requiring content normalization and the larger rejection floor (24).

I would not certify the repository's complete factoring theorem solely from this audit. The remaining work is to integrate that modified sampler, the uniform rational moment bounds, and the bundle-retention recursion into one specification with all constants controlled. The new argument most deserving an independent adversarial check is Lemmas 8–9, especially preservation of smoothness mass through field acceptance.

As finite sanity checks, I verified 3,685 homogeneity/forced-residue identities, 24 nontrivial content normalizations, and 891 exact residue-averaging inequalities. These check identities, not asymptotic probabilities. The independent script and results are in [sanity_checks.py](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/mfU_1/sanity_checks.py) and [sanity_checks.json](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/mfU_1/sanity_checks.json).